In the 1800s, the British-run Great Trigonometrical Survey measured India's mountains using telescopes and careful [[@geometry-everyday|triangles]]. In 1852, a Bengali mathematician named Radhanath Sikdar worked through the calculations and found that a peak known only as Peak XV was the highest in the world. It was later named after George Everest, a former head of the survey. In [[@nepal|Nepal]] it is called Sagarmatha, and in Tibet, Chomolungma.

In 2020, Nepal and China announced a new joint measurement: 8,848.86 m. People first stood on the summit in 1953, when [[@everest-first|Tenzing Norgay and Edmund Hillary]] reached the top.

{{story:bar-headed-goose}}

A hunting bat starts with slow calls to scan the air. Once it locks on to a moth, it calls faster and faster, up to about 200 times a second. Scientists call this the "feeding buzz". The fast calls give the bat a quick stream of updates as the moth dodges. At the last moment, some bats scoop the insect into a pouch of skin between their back legs, or catch it with a wingtip, and eat it in flight.

Some moths fight back. Many can hear bat calls and dive out of the way. Some tiger moths make clicks of their own, which seem to confuse the bat's sonar. How fast sound moves matters here, and you can read more in [[@sound-travel|how sound travels]].

{{story:dolphins-click}}

Fireworks began with gunpowder, a mixture of saltpetre, charcoal and sulphur. Most historians think Chinese alchemists discovered it around the 800s, possibly while searching for a potion for long life. Instead they found a powder that burned in a flash.

People in [[@ancient-china|ancient China]] packed it into bamboo tubes to make loud bangs at festivals, which were believed to scare away evil spirits. Over the following centuries, the knowledge spread west along [[@trade-routes|trade routes]] to the Islamic world, Europe and India. Early fireworks were mostly orange and gold, the colour of burning charcoal and iron sparks. Bright reds, greens and blues only became possible in the 1800s, when chemists learned to make new metal compounds.

{{story:diwali-lights}}

Wood rots, so very old carvings rarely survive. One that did is the Shigir Idol, found by gold miners in a peat bog in Russia's Ural Mountains in 1890. The cold, airless peat kept it safe. Scientists dated it to about 12,000 years ago, when people there still lived by hunting and fishing. It is covered in carved faces and zigzag lines, and its meaning is still a puzzle.

People have carved [[@stone-carving|stone]] for just as long, but wood was easier to find and quicker to shape, so it became the everyday material for bowls, spoons, doors and toys.

{{story:trees-oldest}}

In 1924, two young men named Richard Simon and Max Schuster published a book of crosswords. It came with a pencil attached. It sold so well that it launched their publishing company, Simon & Schuster, which still exists.

Soon crosswords were everywhere. Dictionaries sold out. Train passengers filled in puzzles on their way to work. Some newspapers complained the craze was making people lazy. The *New York Times* called crosswords a "sinful waste" in 1924, then started its own Sunday puzzle in 1942, during the Second World War. People at the paper argued that readers needed something to take their minds off the bad news.

{{story:memory}}

High-speed [[@cameras-how|cameras]] can film thousands of frames a second. Slowed down, they show exactly how a bowler's wrist moves or a sprinter's foot strikes the ground. This study of movement is called biomechanics.

Cricket uses ball-tracking systems such as Hawk-Eye, which use several cameras to work out where a ball went and where it would have gone. Since 2008 these systems have helped umpires make decisions. Coaches also use them to study how bowlers make the ball [[@cricket-swing|swing]] or spin.

{{story:sleep-why}}

Bolt is about 1.95 metres tall, much taller than most sprinters. Tall people usually take longer to get going, and Bolt was often slow out of the blocks. But once he got up to speed, his long legs covered about 2.4 metres with each stride.

In the 100 metres he took about 41 strides, while most rivals took 44 or 45. He also moved his legs very quickly for someone so tall. Sports scientists filmed his races to study his speed, his stride and how long his feet stayed on the ground.

{{story:speed-distance}}

Short sight, or myopia, means faraway things look blurry. It's becoming much more common. In some cities in East Asia, a large majority of young adults are short-sighted. Scientists have estimated that by 2050, about half the people in the world could be.

Close-up work, including screens and books, seems to play a part. But the strongest finding is about daylight. Children who spend more time outdoors are less likely to become short-sighted, whatever else they do. Bright daylight seems to help the eye grow to the right length. When Taiwan began sending schoolchildren outdoors for two hours a day, rates fell. Short-sighted children see clearly again with [[@lenses-glasses|glasses]].

{{story:lenses-glasses}}

In 1966, Seymour Papert at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) wrote a short plan for a "summer vision project". Students would build a system that could pick out objects in a picture. It sounded simple, because seeing feels easy to us.

It wasn't. Your brain uses a huge share of its power on vision, and most of that work happens without you noticing. You see a chair whether it's in sunlight or shadow, half-hidden or upside down. Early programs were thrown off by a change in lighting. For decades, computer vision worked only in tidy conditions, such as reading handwritten postcodes on envelopes or checking parts on a factory line. Engineers tried writing rules by hand: a face has two dark spots for eyes above a line for a mouth. Real faces, in real light, kept breaking the rules.

{{story:eyes-see}}

For centuries, people with hearing loss used ear trumpets: horns that collect sound and funnel it into the ear. [[@beethoven|Beethoven]], who slowly went deaf, owned several. Electric hearing aids appeared in the early 1900s, but they were boxes as big as a radio, with heavy batteries.

{{story:beethoven}}

[[transistor|Transistors]] in the 1950s made hearing aids small enough to wear behind the ear. Today's are digital. They can tell speech from [[@noise-cancelling|background noise]], turn down the clatter of a restaurant and turn up a friend's voice. Many connect by [[@bluetooth|Bluetooth]] to phones and TVs, sending calls and music straight into the ear.

Until 1965 the United States allowed very few immigrants from Asia. A new law that year removed those limits and favoured people with skills. Indian doctors, scientists and engineers began moving to the United States for further study, often a master's degree, then stayed to work.

From the 1990s, special work visas brought tens of thousands more Indian engineers each year, many to work in software. Speaking [[@english-spread|English]], which India's schools and universities had used since British rule, made the move much easier. Their families joined them, and their children grew up as Americans, one strand among the many [[@immigration-stories|immigration stories]] that shaped the country.

{{story:indian-diaspora}}

On 25 August 1896, Sultan Hamad bin Thuwaini died suddenly. Within hours his cousin, Khalid bin Barghash, moved into the palace and declared himself sultan. The British wanted someone else, Hamud bin Muhammed, who they thought would do as they asked.

Khalid gathered a few thousand guards, servants and armed supporters at the palace. The British gathered warships in the harbour. They sent an ultimatum: leave by 9 o'clock on the morning of the 27th, or be fired on.

{{story:spices}}

When India broke away from Gondwana and began its race north, as you can read in [[@india-drift|India's drift]], Australia stayed attached to Antarctica for much longer. When it did split off, it carried its passengers with it like a raft.

At that time, marsupials and egg-laying mammals were already living there. Very few other mammals ever reached the island, except bats, which could fly, and rodents, which probably arrived on floating logs or across short stretches of sea much later. So kangaroos became Australia's grazers, playing the part that deer and antelope play in India or the United States.

{{story:india-drift}}

On top of that same platform, which Muslims call the Noble Sanctuary and Jews call the Temple Mount, stand two important Islamic buildings. The Dome of the Rock, finished around 691 CE, covers a rock that is sacred in both Islam and Judaism. Its golden dome is the most famous sight on the city skyline.

Next to it is the Al-Aqsa Mosque. Muslims believe the Prophet Muhammad was carried here in one night on his Night Journey, before rising to heaven. In the earliest years of Islam, Muslims faced Jerusalem when they prayed, before the direction changed to [[@mecca-hajj|Mecca]].

{{story:archaeology}}

Dickens's first big success, The Pickwick Papers, came out in 1836 and 1837 in monthly parts. Each part was a slim booklet with green paper covers and two pictures. It cost one shilling, much less than a full book.

That way of publishing made reading cheaper, and it made Dickens a celebrity. Families who couldn't afford books would club together or listen as one person read aloud. He also published stories in weekly magazines that he edited himself. Writing in parts meant ending chapters on cliffhangers, a trick now used by every TV drama.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

New emojis are approved by the Unicode Consortium, a non-profit group of technology companies and experts based in California. Anyone can send in a proposal, explaining why an emoji is needed and how often people would use it. Each year a few dozen new ones are accepted, and phone companies draw their own versions.

In 2019, the new emojis included a diya lamp, a sari, an auto rickshaw and a Hindu temple. People in India had long asked for symbols from their daily lives. Emojis have also become more inclusive over time, with different skin tones, wheelchairs, [[@hearing-aids|hearing aids]] and many kinds of families.

